          "reasoning": "**Analyzing app behavior**\n\nI need to address the app's \"read_only\" status, which could be causing the UI to disable modify/delete functions. It would be ideal to change this to \"editable_by_current_user\" or set it to true for superusers. I shouldn't assume field names, so it's important to verify that the UI capability indicators are present and correct, or to remove the server-side \"read-only\" for superusers. Also, I must make sure we explicitly state any unchanged shared API facts.**Creating endpoint tests**\n\nI need to set up unit tests for various endpoint scenarios to verify their status codes. For instance, if a superuser reads, modifies, or deletes another user's resource, I expect responses of 200 or 204, depending on the action. In contrast, regular users should get 404 when they attempt to access or modify another user's resource. Also, I need to ensure that the response body properly indicates the edit capabilities based on user roles and that authorization middleware is in place. Finally, I'll check if any database constraints or triggers need consideration and update the OpenAPI specification if necessary.**Formulating backend instructions**\n\nI need to be cautious about what I include. The guidelines say to avoid mentioning any historical information or guessing details about contracts. I shouldn't specify code functions, frameworks, or field names but focus on abstract terms like \"authorization middleware\" and endpoint types. It's fine to use camelCase for variables. I'll return a JSON response with step-by-step backend implementation instructions in Chinese, along with explicit constraints, test cases, and a deployment checklist. Importantly, I won't instruct any frontend changes.",
